WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.360
YURI GRINSHTEYN: Welcome back.

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:03.620
During our last visit, we
worked with Stackdriver

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:06.420
to set up SLI monitoring
for application latency.

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:09.180
Today, we're going to see what
happens when our application

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:10.620
has a latency issue.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:12.570
We'll diagnose and
treat the problem

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:14.830
and restore our
service back to health.

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:16.866
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:00:16.866 --> 00:00:19.788
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:24.790
Now, you may recall that
the last time you were here,

00:00:24.790 --> 00:00:26.370
we created an
alerting policy that

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:28.420
would let us know if the
latency of our service

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:30.430
went above our
desired threshold.

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:32.500
Checking in on it,
it looks like there's

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:33.797
an incident in progress.

00:00:33.797 --> 00:00:35.380
It seems that there
is an issue that's

00:00:35.380 --> 00:00:37.310
affecting our response times.

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:39.020
It's time to operate.

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:41.320
Now, before we start
cutting, we should first

00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:43.180
figure out what's going on.

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:44.710
We'll use Trace to do that.

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:47.165
Let's switch to the Trace
list and get right into it.

00:00:47.165 --> 00:00:48.790
There are definitely
some requests that

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:50.440
are taking quite a long time.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:52.270
Let's pick one of
these and see what

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:53.755
could be causing our problem.

00:00:53.755 --> 00:00:55.180
Ah, that explains it.

00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:57.360
We can see that when
the request is slow,

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:00.520
it's because we're making a call
to the same service repeatedly.

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:01.890
This seems like a bug.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:05.110
But let's compare that to what
happens when things are fast.

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:08.350
As expected, we're only
calling that service once.

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:11.320
Now we have enough information
to go back to our engineering

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:13.048
team and let them
know that they've

00:01:13.048 --> 00:01:15.340
managed to introduce an
intermittent bug in the service

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:16.210
code.

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:19.210
Meanwhile, we'll roll
back the last change

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:22.630
and see if that restores
performance to what we expect.

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:24.880
The incident is resolved,
and our application

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:26.110
is healthy again.

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:27.880
I'm glad we sorted that out.

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:31.000
Now, you may be asking
yourself, Stackdriver Trace

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:32.260
sure seems great.

00:01:32.260 --> 00:01:34.950
But how can I get my
service calls traced?

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:36.993
Well, let's take a look.

00:01:36.993 --> 00:01:39.160
No matter which language
your service is written in,

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:42.610
it's easy to install Trace and
get your code instrumented.

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:45.490
If your app is in a language
that supports the Trace agent,

00:01:45.490 --> 00:01:49.300
it's as easy as importing the
package and starting the agent.

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:52.530
Another option is to use
the OpenCensus library.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:54.615
Now, you may recall that
this is the same library

00:01:54.615 --> 00:01:56.240
we used for performance
instrumentation

00:01:56.240 --> 00:01:57.725
the last time around.

00:01:57.725 --> 00:02:00.100
If you would like a single
method of instrumentation that

00:02:00.100 --> 00:02:03.160
combines both performance
metrics and tracing,

00:02:03.160 --> 00:02:04.700
this is a great way to go.

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:07.390
And finally, lots of folks
like the Zipkin open source

00:02:07.390 --> 00:02:08.530
tracing system.

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:10.270
It's based on the
Dapper research paper

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:12.520
that Google published
back in 2010.

00:02:12.520 --> 00:02:15.380
And Google still uses the
Dapper framework internally.

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:17.930
No matter which instrumentation
option you choose,

00:02:17.930 --> 00:02:19.960
you can use Stackdriver
as the back end.

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:22.720
It's great to have a consistent
way of analyzing tracing,

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:25.070
no matter where it comes from.

00:02:25.070 --> 00:02:27.190
Now that we've covered
Stackdriver Trace and all

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:29.810
the different ways you can
get it to work with your code,

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:31.660
you'll be much better
prepared to tackle

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:35.170
any latency issues that may
come up in your own service.

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:37.130
Next time, we'll
profile the code

00:02:37.130 --> 00:02:39.160
to figure out exactly
why a particular service

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:40.540
call could be slow.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:41.920
That's it for this episode.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:43.330
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:44.560
Stay healthy out there.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:47.910
[MUSIC PLAYING]

